<p>NOTES: Full company name is HF Ahmanson &amp; Co, parent of Home Savings of America.</p>
<p>EPS grew faster than net income because of the company's stock buy back program.</p>
<p>Q2 results include a post-tax gain of $24.6 million, or $0.22 per fully diluted share, on the sale of the company's deposit branch system in Florida.</p>
<p>Q2 results includes a post-tax cost of $3.2 million, or $0.03 per fully diluted share, as a result of its proposed merger with Great Western Financial Corp, which was withdrawn June 4.</p>
<p>Excluding the gain and the cost above, earnings would have been $94.3 million, or $0.82 per fully diluted share.</p>
<p>Also associated with the withdrawn Great Western proposal are $23.1 million in pretax expenses. Those were largely offset by the sale of 3.6 million Great Western shares, which resulted in a pretax gain of $17.6 million.</p>
<p>Company said improved credit costs, lower expenses and higher fee income more than offset effects of compressed net interest margin to increase earnings. Credit costs dropped 35 percent from the same quarter a year ago.</p>
<p>Net loan charge-offs dropped to $17.4 million from $36.8 million a year ago.</p>
<p>Non-performing assets declined by $102 million.</p>
<p>((--New York Newsdesk 212 859-1610))</p>
